---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const updated = '2026-08-30';
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Oddversity stores, what it does not, and where your reading progress actually lives. No accounts, no tracking cookies, no personal data on any server."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Privacy', url: '/privacy' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header>
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h1>What this site knows about you.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Almost nothing, and this page is specific about which parts of that are a
        design decision and which are simply a consequence of how the site is built.
      </p>
      <p class="updated mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Oddversity is a static site. There is no account system, no login, no
        comment box, no newsletter form and no server-side database holding
        anything about readers. Nothing you do here is associated with an
        identity, because there is no identity to associate it with.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored, and where</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Reading progress.</strong> When you mark a lesson complete, the site
        writes a record to <code>localStorage</code> in your own browser. It holds
        which lesson identifiers you marked and when, the last lesson you opened
        so the site can offer to resume it, the last fifteen lessons you opened,
        and any lessons you saved for later. All of it is listed on
        <a href="/saved">the Saved page</a>, which can also export it as plain text
        so you can paste it into another browser yourself. That data never leaves
        your device — there is no endpoint it could be sent to.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Theme preference.</strong> Light or dark, also in
        <code>localStorage</code>, so the page does not flash the wrong colours on
        your next visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Clearing your browser's site data for this domain erases both, permanently
        and completely. Using a different browser or device means starting from
        scratch, because nothing is synced anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        The site sets none. Not for analytics, not for preferences, not for
        anything. <code>localStorage</code> is not a cookie: it is never attached to
        a request, so it cannot be read by this server or by anyone else.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      {site.analytics.provider ? (
        <p>
          The site uses <strong>{site.analytics.provider}</strong>, configured
          without cookies and without cross-site identifiers. It records aggregate
          page views and referrers. It does not build a profile of you, follow you
          to other sites, or collect anything that identifies you individually.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          There is currently <strong>no analytics script of any kind</strong>. No
          page views are counted, no referrers recorded, nothing. If that changes,
          it will be a cookieless, aggregate-only tool and this page will say which
          one and what it collects, before it ships.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>Third parties</h2>
      <p>
        There are none in the page. Fonts are self-hosted on this domain rather than
        loaded from a font CDN, so no third party sees your IP address as a
        side effect of rendering text. There are no embedded videos, no tag
        managers, no chat widgets, no advertising scripts and no external images.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two ordinary exceptions apply. Your DNS resolver and the hosting provider
        serving these files necessarily see the request, as they would for any
        website. And if you follow an outbound link, you are on someone else's site
        under their policy.
      </p>

      <h2>Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The host keeps standard request logs — IP address, timestamp, path, user
        agent — for operational and abuse-prevention purposes, as every web server
        does. They are not joined to anything else, not used to build profiles, and
        not sold or shared.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        The site is written for a general adult and professional audience. It does
        not knowingly collect information from anyone, of any age, because it does
        not collect information at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Data-protection law gives you rights of access, correction, deletion and
        portability over personal data held about you. In this case there is nothing
        to exercise them against on our side: no personal data is held. The only
        data that exists is in your browser, and the delete button is your browser's
        own "clear site data".
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. Anything that
        would newly collect data — analytics, a newsletter, accounts — will be
        described here before it is turned on, not after.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      {site.contact.email ? (
        <p>Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${site.contact.email}`}>{site.contact.email}</a>.</p>
      ) : (
        <p>Questions about this policy can be raised through the channels listed on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.</p>
      )}
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: 720px; margin-inline: auto; padding-block: 56px 8px; padding-inline: 32px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 42px); letter-spacing: -.022em; margin-top: 12px; }
  .lede { margin-top: 18px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); }
  .updated { margin-top: 14px; font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose { margin-top: 36px; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .wrap { padding-block: 36px 8px; padding-inline: 20px; } }
</style>
